# Rate Limiting Rules
## Purpose
Defines **mandatory rate limiting standards** to prevent abuse and ensure fair resource usage.
Ensures:
- API abuse prevention
- Fair resource distribution
- DDoS protection
- Configurable limits per endpoint/user
Any violation is **invalid**.
---

## Configuration
- Rate limiting configuration **must** be defined in `src/config/rateLimit.js` (per `project-structure.mdc`)
- Rate limiting middleware **must** be applied globally in `src/app/index.js`
- Configuration **must** be environment-aware (different limits for dev/staging/production)
---

## Default Limits
- **Global limit**: 100 requests per 15 minutes per IP (configurable)
- **Authenticated users**: Higher limits (e.g., 1000 requests per 15 minutes)
- **Specific endpoints** may have custom limits:
  - Authentication endpoints: 5 requests per 15 minutes per IP
  - File upload endpoints: 10 requests per hour per user
  - Search endpoints: 60 requests per minute per user
---

## Rate Limit Headers
- Rate limit responses **must** include standard headers:
  - `X-RateLimit-Limit`: Maximum requests allowed
  - `X-RateLimit-Remaining`: Remaining requests in window
  - `X-RateLimit-Reset`: Unix timestamp when limit resets
- Exceeding limit **must** return 429 Too Many Requests
- Error response **must** follow standard format (per `response-format.mdc`)
---

## Storage & Implementation
- Rate limit data **should** be stored in Redis for distributed systems
- In-memory storage **may** be used for single-instance deployments
- Rate limiting **must** use token bucket or sliding window algorithm
- Rate limit middleware **must** be applied before route handlers
---

## Per-User Limits
- Authenticated users **must** be rate-limited by user ID, not just IP
- User-based limits **must** be higher than IP-based limits
- Rate limits **must** reset per user, not globally
---

## Endpoint-Specific Limits
- Critical endpoints **must** have stricter limits:
  - Authentication endpoints: 5 attempts per 15 minutes per IP
  - Registration endpoints: 3 attempts per hour per IP
  - Password reset endpoints: 3 attempts per hour per IP
- File upload endpoints **must** have lower limits to prevent abuse
- Search endpoints **may** have higher limits for better UX
---

## Error Response
- Rate limit exceeded **must** return:
  - Status: 429 Too Many Requests
  - Message: "Rate limit exceeded. Please try again later."
  - Include reset time in response body
- Response format:
  ```javascript
  {
    "status": 429,
    "message": "Rate limit exceeded. Please try again later.",
    "data": null,
    "errors": [{
      "field": "rate_limit",
      "message": "Limit will reset at <timestamp>"
    }]
  }
  ```
---

## Logging & Monitoring
- Rate limit violations **must** be logged
- Repeated violations from same IP/user **must** trigger alerts
- Rate limit metrics **should** be collected for monitoring
- Patterns of abuse **must** be flagged for security review
---

## Testing
- Rate limiting **must** be tested in integration tests
- Tests **must** verify limit enforcement and header responses
- Edge cases (limit reset, concurrent requests) **must** be tested
---

## Forbidden Patterns
- Bypassing rate limits for any endpoint
- Not including rate limit headers in responses
- Using same limits for all endpoints without consideration
- Ignoring rate limit violations in logs
---

## Enforcement
❌ Violations are invalid  
✅ Rate limiting must be fair, configurable, and properly monitored  
All rate limiting **must strictly follow these rules**.
